WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.832
This is your first more independent table-writing exercise.

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:06.917
Do not start from a blank screen mentally.

00:00:06.917 --> 00:00:10.657
Use the comments and earlier examples as a pattern.

00:00:10.657 --> 00:00:14.627
Decide what one row in your small table represents.

00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:22.712
Choose a primary key, identify which columns are required, and add a simple CHECK or other rule where

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:27.652
it makes sense. Then insert one valid row and run a SELECT to inspect it.

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:32.287
Use the browser editor and run your code in small steps if needed.

00:00:32.287 --> 00:00:37.802
If PostgreSQL reports an error, read it carefully and fix one issue at a time.

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:42.717
The slide includes one possible answer, but try your own version first.

00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:47.682
The purpose is to practice translating a small design into executable PostgreSQL,

00:00:47.682 --> 00:00:50.457
not to reproduce one exact script.

00:00:50.457 --> 00:00:57.117
Connect this idea to an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines.

00:00:57.117 --> 00:01:02.687
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:10.012
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:10.012 --> 00:01:15.812
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:21.852
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
